WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:03.599
Welcome to Module 1 of the
Ticket-to-Machine learning path:

2
00:00:03.599 --> 00:00:05.225
the loop and the typed task.

3
00:00:05.225 --> 00:00:10.280
This path builds the identity half of the
Federal Modernization Workbook's Book 13's

4
00:00:10.280 --> 00:00:14.684
Ticket-to-Machine loop on two named
products: ServiceNow as the front door

5
00:00:14.684 --> 00:00:19.450
and the record, and SailPoint Identity
Security Cloud for identity governance.

6
00:00:19.450 --> 00:00:21.888
They're an example, not an endorsement.

7
00:00:21.888 --> 00:00:25.905
This module starts at the guide's
Gate 4, because every other gate

8
00:00:25.905 --> 00:00:30.075
writes or reads the record that
gate defines: the identity finding.

9
00:00:30.075 --> 00:00:32.952
You'll map the kit's files
to the loop's five steps,

10
00:00:32.952 --> 00:00:36.862
learn the finding's data contract,
and check payloads on a laptop

11
00:00:36.862 --> 00:00:40.980
the way ServiceNow will, before
anything reaches an instance.

12
00:00:44.730 --> 00:00:46.067
Why start with a record?

13
00:00:46.067 --> 00:00:48.379
Picture your agency's service desk.

14
00:00:48.379 --> 00:00:51.580
An access ticket closes when
a technician types done.

15
00:00:51.580 --> 00:00:54.418
Nobody can say who owned
the drift, when it fell due,

16
00:00:54.418 --> 00:00:57.117
or what the account should
look like afterward.

17
00:00:57.117 --> 00:01:01.503
Book 13 says an assessor asks four
questions about every finding:

18
00:01:01.503 --> 00:01:06.456
who owns it, by when, under whose approval,
and with what evidence of closure.

19
00:01:06.456 --> 00:01:10.655
A ticket closed on done answers
none of them; it records a claim.

20
00:01:10.655 --> 00:01:15.581
Book 13's answer is a typed task:
one record with a class, a severity,

21
00:01:15.581 --> 00:01:20.692
an owner, a clock, and an intended
state that a later re-read can check.

22
00:01:20.692 --> 00:01:22.831
Approval comes in later modules.

23
00:01:22.831 --> 00:01:27.700
Every step of the loop writes or reads
this record, so it's where you start.

24
00:01:31.450 --> 00:01:34.261
Here's what you'll be able to
do by the end of this module,

25
00:01:34.261 --> 00:01:36.625
which takes about fifty
minutes with the lab.

26
00:01:36.625 --> 00:01:39.228
You'll explain the loop's
five steps and name

27
00:01:39.228 --> 00:01:41.687
the file in the kit
that performs each one.

28
00:01:41.687 --> 00:01:44.182
You'll describe the fields of the identity

29
00:01:44.182 --> 00:01:46.962
finding's data contract
and what each one is for.

30
00:01:46.962 --> 00:01:50.889
You'll validate a finding payload
on a laptop, and predict whether

31
00:01:50.889 --> 00:01:55.325
ServiceNow accepts it, rejects it or
de-duplicates it, and when it falls due.

32
00:01:55.325 --> 00:01:59.900
And you'll recognize an intended state
that gives the re-read nothing to check.

33
00:01:59.900 --> 00:02:04.725
Before you start, read Book 13's
Chapter 10 and skim Appendix B.

34
00:02:04.725 --> 00:02:09.663
For Lab 1 you need Python, Node.js
and a Bash-compatible shell.

35
00:02:09.663 --> 00:02:14.370
You don't need a ServiceNow instance
or a SailPoint tenant until Lab 2.

36
00:02:18.120 --> 00:02:19.695
The loop has five steps.

37
00:02:19.695 --> 00:02:25.195
One, test: a rule evaluates actual
state against the authoritative record.

38
00:02:25.195 --> 00:02:29.932
Two, detect drift: a difference becomes
a typed finding with a severity.

39
00:02:29.932 --> 00:02:34.384
Three, raise a typed task against
the reconciled configuration item,

40
00:02:34.384 --> 00:02:35.946
with an owner and a clock.

41
00:02:35.946 --> 00:02:38.272
Four, actuate on the native surface,

42
00:02:38.272 --> 00:02:42.445
under a service identity, through
the platform's own interface.

43
00:02:42.445 --> 00:02:44.297
And five, close with evidence:

44
00:02:44.297 --> 00:02:48.620
re-read the post-change state and
write one record of the whole chain.

45
00:02:48.620 --> 00:02:51.164
The coordination layer
in front of the native

46
00:02:51.164 --> 00:02:53.708
tools never writes the
configuration itself.

47
00:02:53.708 --> 00:02:56.291
Its job is to make every drift an owned,

48
00:02:56.291 --> 00:02:59.458
clocked, approved and
evidenced piece of work.

49
00:02:59.458 --> 00:03:02.899
In the program's model, the
ticket authorizes a machine,

50
00:03:02.899 --> 00:03:05.630
and the person's role ends at approval.

51
00:03:09.380 --> 00:03:11.243
Now map the kit onto that loop.

52
00:03:11.243 --> 00:03:15.628
Step one, test, is the drift
client, which joins the HR extract,

53
00:03:15.628 --> 00:03:19.028
Microsoft Graph and
SailPoint on employee I D.

54
00:03:19.028 --> 00:03:22.876
Step two, detect drift, is
SailPoint's event triggers,

55
00:03:22.876 --> 00:03:27.618
for a native change or an attribute
change, plus the drift client.

56
00:03:27.618 --> 00:03:32.845
Step three, raise a typed task, is the
SailPoint workflows' HTTP Request step and

57
00:03:32.845 --> 00:03:38.393
the Scripted REST resource in ServiceNow,
which writes the identity finding table.

58
00:03:38.393 --> 00:03:43.457
Step five, close with evidence, is a
business rule that turns any close into

59
00:03:43.457 --> 00:03:48.605
Awaiting verification, and the re-read
client, which closes only on a re-read.

60
00:03:48.605 --> 00:03:49.843
Notice step four.

61
00:03:49.843 --> 00:03:52.150
Actuation has no code in Pass 1:

62
00:03:52.150 --> 00:03:57.318
nothing in the kit writes to Entra
ID, Active Directory or SailPoint.

63
00:03:57.318 --> 00:04:00.200
You'll meet that step again in Module 6.

64
00:04:03.950 --> 00:04:07.175
The example guide shows
which product does each step.

65
00:04:07.175 --> 00:04:09.520
For raising a typed task, which is Gate 4,

66
00:04:09.520 --> 00:04:14.062
it's a SailPoint workflow posting to a
ServiceNow endpoint, which is a build;

67
00:04:14.062 --> 00:04:16.846
the Service Catalog app
for people's requests;

68
00:04:16.846 --> 00:04:20.363
and Service Desk tickets for
disconnected applications.

69
00:04:20.363 --> 00:04:24.974
Used as shipped, the two products fall
short of the loop in four places.

70
00:04:24.974 --> 00:04:29.479
One: SailPoint's Service Desk
integration treats its task as complete

71
00:04:29.479 --> 00:04:33.362
from the ServiceNow status, and
a ticket status isn't a re-read.

72
00:04:33.362 --> 00:04:37.724
Two: the catalog app moves
access approval into ServiceNow.

73
00:04:37.724 --> 00:04:41.482
Three: SailPoint's Service
Desk integration raises only

74
00:04:41.482 --> 00:04:45.000
service requests and incidents,
not change requests.

75
00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:48.125
And four: there are two
doors into the directory.

76
00:04:48.125 --> 00:04:52.460
Each gate that closes a gap
says so; the slide shows which.

77
00:04:56.210 --> 00:05:01.272
Here's the record those steps share: the
identity finding, and its data contract.

78
00:05:01.272 --> 00:05:05.022
Kind is a finding, the
default, or a closure signal.

79
00:05:05.022 --> 00:05:08.135
Source is SailPoint or
the drift reconciliation.

80
00:05:08.135 --> 00:05:13.073
The drift class is one of Book 03's
seven classes, and it's required.

81
00:05:13.073 --> 00:05:18.022
Severity is P one to P four; leave
it out and it defaults by class,

82
00:05:18.022 --> 00:05:23.360
Book 03's High to P two and Medium to
P three, which is design guidance.

83
00:05:23.360 --> 00:05:25.945
System says where the re-read happens:

84
00:05:25.945 --> 00:05:29.948
a SailPoint account, a SailPoint
identity or an Entra user.

85
00:05:29.948 --> 00:05:35.973
One subject identifier is required, and
de-duplication uses the first one present.

86
00:05:35.973 --> 00:05:37.622
Origin says what raised it.

87
00:05:37.622 --> 00:05:40.930
And the intended state says
what a re-read must find;

88
00:05:40.930 --> 00:05:45.184
when a SailPoint event arrives
without one, the script derives it.

89
00:05:45.184 --> 00:05:50.460
The implementation example maps each
field to Book 13's evidence record.

90
00:05:54.210 --> 00:05:56.712
The contract is enforced in one place:

91
00:05:56.712 --> 00:06:00.585
the Scripted REST resource,
which runs inside ServiceNow.

92
00:06:00.585 --> 00:06:02.903
It validates the payload and answers four

93
00:06:02.903 --> 00:06:05.485
hundred with the reason
when something's wrong.

94
00:06:05.485 --> 00:06:11.110
It maps severity to a priority, one to
four, and a due date on the program's tempo.

95
00:06:11.110 --> 00:06:15.990
It de-duplicates against an active finding:
when one already has the same source,

96
00:06:15.990 --> 00:06:19.170
subject, class and attribute,
it answers two hundred

97
00:06:19.170 --> 00:06:22.497
with the existing record
instead of creating a second.

98
00:06:22.497 --> 00:06:27.335
And when a SailPoint event arrives with
no intended state, it derives one.

99
00:06:27.335 --> 00:06:31.310
A new finding gets two oh one,
with its number and priority.

100
00:06:31.310 --> 00:06:33.973
Here's the problem Chapter 01 solved.

101
00:06:33.973 --> 00:06:38.255
Until then, nothing outside ServiceNow
applied the same rules, so a client

102
00:06:38.255 --> 00:06:42.960
learned that a payload was wrong only
from a four hundred on a live instance.

103
00:06:46.710 --> 00:06:48.310
Where do the due dates come from?

104
00:06:48.310 --> 00:06:51.172
Book 13's severity and tempo table.

105
00:06:51.172 --> 00:06:54.329
A P one, critical, isn't
fixed by a machine:

106
00:06:54.329 --> 00:06:58.273
the run halts and alerts,
and a named role decides.

107
00:06:58.273 --> 00:07:00.110
Its window is immediate.

108
00:07:00.110 --> 00:07:03.148
P two, high, has a one-hour window.

109
00:07:03.148 --> 00:07:06.010
P three, medium, twenty-four hours.

110
00:07:06.010 --> 00:07:09.148
And P four, low, seventy-two hours.

111
00:07:09.148 --> 00:07:12.786
The script turns those windows
into each record's due date.

112
00:07:12.786 --> 00:07:13.948
Two cautions.

113
00:07:13.948 --> 00:07:19.110
These are the program's internal windows,
program design, not a federal requirement.

114
00:07:19.110 --> 00:07:21.835
They bound the response,
not the occurrence.

115
00:07:21.835 --> 00:07:24.538
And they don't replace external timelines:

116
00:07:24.538 --> 00:07:29.173
where a CISA directive sets a shorter
clock, the external clock governs.

117
00:07:29.173 --> 00:07:35.180
Book 13 also notes that a program with no
findings is more likely blind than clean.

118
00:07:38.930 --> 00:07:41.842
Chapter 01 adds finding contract dot py,

119
00:07:41.842 --> 00:07:45.018
which applies the script's
rules on a laptop.

120
00:07:45.018 --> 00:07:50.547
Its validate function returns the errors
ServiceNow would answer four hundred to,

121
00:07:50.547 --> 00:07:55.916
warnings for what it would accept but the
loop can't use, and the normalized values:

122
00:07:55.916 --> 00:08:00.243
severity, priority, system, subject,
de-duplication key and window.

123
00:08:00.243 --> 00:08:02.375
Its constants mirror the script's,

124
00:08:02.375 --> 00:08:06.181
and a test reads the JavaScript
and fails if they drift apart.

125
00:08:06.181 --> 00:08:07.530
Then there's is checkable.

126
00:08:07.530 --> 00:08:11.128
An intended state of empty
attributes and an empty list

127
00:08:11.128 --> 00:08:14.805
of entitlements is a non-empty
object, so it looks present.

128
00:08:14.805 --> 00:08:19.305
But a re-read finds no difference against
it, and would close the record on nothing.

129
00:08:19.305 --> 00:08:20.655
So the check warns.

130
00:08:20.655 --> 00:08:26.375
For missing identity and unauthorized write,
which Book 13 routes to a person first,

131
00:08:26.375 --> 00:08:30.470
the empty intent is expected
until that person sets it.

132
00:08:34.220 --> 00:08:34.945
Try it.

133
00:08:34.945 --> 00:08:37.857
Lab 1 runs in the kit's
folder, on a laptop,

134
00:08:37.857 --> 00:08:41.133
against fictional
fixtures, with no tenant.

135
00:08:41.133 --> 00:08:45.945
In step two you check five sample payloads
the way the Scripted REST resource would.

136
00:08:45.945 --> 00:08:49.857
Sample one gave no severity,
so the class default gave it P

137
00:08:49.857 --> 00:08:53.770
two and a one-hour window: due at
one o'clock for a run at noon.

138
00:08:53.770 --> 00:08:59.420
Sample two has a typo in its drift class,
and sample four has a severity of P five.

139
00:08:59.420 --> 00:09:02.770
Both would get a four hundred,
so the command exits one.

140
00:09:02.770 --> 00:09:07.489
Sample three is accepted, but warned:
its intended state names nothing

141
00:09:07.489 --> 00:09:11.720
a re-read can check, which is
expected for an unauthorized write.

142
00:09:11.720 --> 00:09:17.332
And sample five, a closure signal, moves
matching findings to Awaiting verification.

143
00:09:17.332 --> 00:09:19.040
It never closes them.

144
00:09:22.790 --> 00:09:26.453
Step one runs the chapter's
tests: twenty-two pass.

145
00:09:26.453 --> 00:09:30.074
Step three runs the ServiceNow
script's own tests,

146
00:09:30.074 --> 00:09:35.727
the rules the Python mirrors, under Node:
fifteen tests, fifteen pass, none fail.

147
00:09:35.727 --> 00:09:38.786
Then step four runs the
drift reconciliation over

148
00:09:38.786 --> 00:09:41.916
the fixtures and checks
every payload it would post.

149
00:09:41.916 --> 00:09:43.528
Twelve lines start ok.

150
00:09:43.528 --> 00:09:48.721
Attribute drift is P three, due in
twenty-four hours; missing identities,

151
00:09:48.721 --> 00:09:53.915
orphans, stale access and the unauthorized
write are P two, due in an hour.

152
00:09:53.915 --> 00:09:59.069
Three carry the warning: the two missing
identities and the unauthorized write,

153
00:09:59.069 --> 00:10:01.490
the classes Book 13 routes to a person.

154
00:10:01.490 --> 00:10:06.365
And the last line is twelve accepted,
none rejected, none de-duplicated.

155
00:10:06.365 --> 00:10:12.177
Erin's two drifts in Entra ID, her department
and her manager, have their own keys.

156
00:10:12.177 --> 00:10:15.020
Step five deletes the output folder.

157
00:10:18.770 --> 00:10:23.620
Lab 2 moves to a ServiceNow Personal
Developer Instance, never production.

158
00:10:23.620 --> 00:10:28.107
You need the web service admin role
to create the Scripted REST API.

159
00:10:28.107 --> 00:10:31.612
You build the finding table
and the Scripted REST resource,

160
00:10:31.612 --> 00:10:35.332
and lock the resource down with
a REST endpoint access control.

161
00:10:35.332 --> 00:10:40.058
Then you post a payload you already
checked in Lab 1, and confirm two things on

162
00:10:40.058 --> 00:10:45.008
the instance: a repeat de-duplicates, and
a malformed payload gets a four hundred.

163
00:10:45.008 --> 00:10:50.380
Back it out before you leave the module:
step five of the chapter's Lab 2 deletes

164
00:10:50.380 --> 00:10:55.920
the test records, the access control, the
role, the API and the scoped application.

165
00:10:55.920 --> 00:10:57.804
The authors haven't run Lab 2,

166
00:10:57.804 --> 00:11:02.680
and the workbook has no hosted
sandbox: you bring your own instance.

167
00:11:06.430 --> 00:11:07.817
The federal requirement.

168
00:11:07.817 --> 00:11:13.443
Gate 4 turns findings and requests into
documented, owned, clocked records.

169
00:11:13.443 --> 00:11:15.820
The guide maps it to four controls in NIST

170
00:11:15.820 --> 00:11:18.617
special publication eight
hundred fifty-three.

171
00:11:18.617 --> 00:11:21.538
C M three, configuration change control:

172
00:11:21.538 --> 00:11:25.343
every proposed change is a
documented, owned record.

173
00:11:25.343 --> 00:11:30.605
R A seven, risk response: findings
are answered inside declared windows.

174
00:11:30.605 --> 00:11:35.755
C A seven, continuous monitoring:
monitoring results become tracked work.

175
00:11:35.755 --> 00:11:39.430
And A C five, separation of
duties: a request passes a

176
00:11:39.430 --> 00:11:44.243
separation-of-duties check and an
approver who isn't the requester.

177
00:11:44.243 --> 00:11:47.281
The evidence an ISSO
keeps from this module:

178
00:11:47.281 --> 00:11:52.431
the ServiceNow finding records and
the contract tests in the repository,

179
00:11:52.431 --> 00:11:55.892
and each record's due date,
which Module 7 measures.

180
00:11:55.892 --> 00:11:59.340
Before shipping, work through
the chapter's checklist.

181
00:12:03.090 --> 00:12:06.590
Check yourself with two questions
from the module's knowledge check.

182
00:12:06.590 --> 00:12:11.303
First: a payload has a drift class
of stale access and no severity.

183
00:12:11.303 --> 00:12:13.627
What does the Scripted REST resource do?

184
00:12:13.627 --> 00:12:14.840
The answer is c.

185
00:12:14.840 --> 00:12:19.065
It accepts it with the class default,
P two, and a one-hour window.

186
00:12:19.065 --> 00:12:21.483
The script defaults severity by class,

187
00:12:21.483 --> 00:12:24.278
and sample one in the
lab showed exactly that.

188
00:12:24.278 --> 00:12:28.428
Second: what does a closure signal
payload do to matching findings?

189
00:12:28.428 --> 00:12:29.652
The answer is b.

190
00:12:29.652 --> 00:12:33.416
It moves them to Awaiting
verification, and it never closes them.

191
00:12:33.416 --> 00:12:35.690
Only a re-read closes a finding.

192
00:12:35.690 --> 00:12:40.950
The module page has two more questions,
each linked to the section that answers it.

193
00:12:44.700 --> 00:12:45.525
To sum up.

194
00:12:45.525 --> 00:12:48.159
You mapped the kit to
the loop's five steps,

195
00:12:48.159 --> 00:12:51.025
and found that actuation
has no code in Pass 1.

196
00:12:51.025 --> 00:12:55.875
You read the identity finding's contract,
and the tempo its due dates come from.

197
00:12:55.875 --> 00:12:59.222
You checked payloads on a
laptop the way ServiceNow will,

198
00:12:59.222 --> 00:13:02.213
and then every payload the
drift client would post.

199
00:13:02.213 --> 00:13:05.468
Before you ship, work through
Chapter 01's checklist:

200
00:13:05.468 --> 00:13:08.043
the constants match the deployed script,

201
00:13:08.043 --> 00:13:12.739
every client runs its payloads through
the check, and the Scripted REST API

202
00:13:12.739 --> 00:13:17.737
requires authentication and a REST endpoint
access control with a custom role.

203
00:13:17.737 --> 00:13:20.936
Next is Module 2, the
boundary and least privilege:

204
00:13:20.936 --> 00:13:25.852
Gate 1's record of what runs where, and
the ServiceNow roles, SailPoint scopes

205
00:13:25.852 --> 00:13:30.680
and Graph permissions each task needs,
checked against the kit's call list.
